  "problem": "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\,{y}^{\u2032}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$",
